25f709b716f8273b349524e5613e0a1ad540937b464e88a4025696ac" "basis" 1374 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039569/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-77478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\n- $R$ is a singular matrix\n- $det(R)=0$\n- $det(R)=1$\n\n \n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039569/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc836edc25f709b716f8273b349524e5613e0a1ad540937b464e88a4025696ac" "basis" 1374 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039569/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-77478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|-\\frac{2}{5}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{5}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(2-\\frac{3}{2})-\\frac{2}{5}(1-0)+\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(R)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" 